.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.chapter 8
.h1 Organisation of the work [machines]
Work ran in parallel on two machines.  The project maintainer set the goals and
decided.  Assistant sessions, instances of Claude, did the jobs, several at a
time.  This document names no machine and no person.
.h2 Roles [machines-people]
{Tab:roles} lists the three roles of the record.
.tabcap roles Roles in the work
.table 24 40
Role | Tasks
The project maintainer | A human.  Set the goals, answered questions, reviewed
 | documents, grouped and ranked the worklist.  Every decision is in the
 | worklist with its date.
The coordinator | An assistant session.  Kept the main line, scheduled jobs,
 | merged the returned branches, measured and wrote notes across jobs.
The jobs | Assistant sessions.  Each took one task from a task file, worked on a
 | branch of its own and reported.  Jobs do not talk to each other.
.end
The release criterion is an example of a decision: the project maintainer
grouped the worklist items of one document as R1, and no item outside R1
starts unless the project maintainer asks.  Review comments asked that every
statement about memory, speed and disk be measured in pages and words, not
"small".  That request is the origin of {x:memory}.
.h2 Machines [machines-two]
The first machine had 16 threads and a Debian 13 system; the second had 8
threads and a newer kernel.  The first held the text collection, so the
measurements that quote it were taken there.  The second used a text made from
the project documents ({x:memory-survey}).  The second machine took the numbered
backups, the long temporary path, the attribute table and the colour of
pictures, and later steps of the Border and the Section marker.  A task came
as a file and returned as a branch and a report.  Two things made the division
possible: the verification
of a branch showed whether a job broke what other jobs rely on, and every job
received the same rules: the C style, the form of commit messages, and a
feature is finished only with its manual text, help and tests.
.h2 Verification and commits [machines-verify]
A branch is merged when it is verified: the build, the unit tests, the smoke
and the X11 tests pass, and the tree is unchanged ({x:testing-counts}).  The
generated manual is committed alone after each verification, from "after the
merge of five jobs" on 16 November to "after verification 58" on 19 November.
Every commit message has a summary of at most 80 characters, a section
Description with What, How and Why, and a section Testing with the exact
commands and results.
.h2 Unattended runs [machines-overnight]
Tasks were queued and ran unattended.  Of the @commits_to_19@ commits to the end
of 19 November, @commits_night@ were made between midnight and seven in the
morning.  A job follows four rules without asking: decide, and write the
decision in the report; commit and write a progress note every fifteen minutes;
build debug and release, because a release build with strict warnings finds
what a debug build does not; end with a report that lists what was not
finished, the decisions made and the open questions.
The project maintainer reads these lists in the morning.  A wrong decision
stays in until a report is read: the first guess for the level indent
({x:probing-learned}) and the slower save of {x:memory-slower} are examples.
.endif
